{{!-- Which map is showing, and what the GM can do to the picture.

      SHARED BY BOTH SURFACES. The route step draws this above its map; the "See the whole map"
      window draws the same row above its viewport, which is what makes the popout a peer of the
      panel rather than a picture of it. Context is the journey object from
      ExpeditionDialog._buildJourney.

      Every control is bound once, in that dialog's activateListeners, and the window forwards its
      own clicks to the very same methods — so "put a site on the map" cannot come to mean two
      different things depending on which window you did it in.

      THE ROW OF FACTS ABOVE THIS ONE HAS GONE (user, 2026-08-24). It carried "Setting out from
      Stonetop" — a button whose press armed the next click — then "bound for Marshedge", then an ×
      to un-pick that. Three ways of saying what the map already draws, one of them a mode that was
      invisible until you were in it, and the only route out of two of those states.

      THE PICTURE IS THE CONTROL NOW. A click says where they set out from, or where they are
      bound, or moves the end of the way; a shift-click lays a leg; a right-click takes the trip
      back one step at a time — the last mark, then the destination, then the start itself, until
      the map is empty and the next click starts it again. Every state this screen can be put into
      by clicking comes back off by right-clicking, which is what let the row go: nothing here is
      the only way to undo something. See ExpeditionDialog `_undoJourneyMark` for the ladder, and
      expedition-journey-drawhint.hbs for the line under the map that teaches it. --}}
<div class="stonetop-journey-tiers">
  {{!-- The tablist is its own element INSIDE the row rather than being the row, because a tablist
        may hold tabs and nothing else. The buttons beside it are not tabs (they change no panel and
        select nothing), so a reader told "tab 3 of 4" about one would be told something untrue. --}}
  <div class="stonetop-journey-tierlist" role="tablist" aria-label="Map">
    {{#each journey.tiers}}
    <button type="button" role="tab" aria-selected="{{isActive}}" data-tier="{{slug}}"
            class="stonetop-journey-tier{{#if isActive}} is-active{{/if}}">
      {{name}}{{#unless hasMap}} <i class="fas fa-image-slash" data-tooltip="Not imported"></i>{{/unless}}
    </button>
    {{/each}}
  </div>
  {{!-- Put one of the GM's own written-up sites on this map, or write a new one and put THAT on it
        (module/sites/place-site-on-map.js). It rides the end of the map-tab row because what it
        adds is a mark on THIS picture: the chooser is followed by a click on the map showing here,
        so the control belongs beside the tabs that decide which map that is.

        GM-only, and absent rather than disabled for a player: a site is prep filed in a journal
        players cannot see or write. It is also built only once there is a map on screen, since a
        placement is a point on a picture. --}}
  {{#if journey.placeSite}}
  {{!-- SITE_ICON, the mark a site wears in a LIST (module/sites/place-site-on-map.js) and the one
        the chooser this button opens puts beside every row in it. NOT the standing stone a placed
        site wears as a pin on the picture below: that one is drawn at 18px, where this one does
        not read. One idea, two sizes, two drawings -- and this control belongs with the list. --}}
  <button type="button" class="stonetop-journey-place-site" data-tooltip="{{journey.placeSite.tooltip}}">
    <i class="fas fa-mountain-sun"></i> {{journey.placeSite.label}}
  </button>
  {{/if}}
  {{!-- Put the way they go onto the table's own map. NEXT TO "put a site on the map" (user,
        2026-08-24), and it belongs there for the same reason that one does: both are acts on a
        picture rather than facts about the trip. It sat with the destination while there was a
        destination readout to sit with; with that row gone, the two buttons that write something
        onto a map are the row.

        The scene it draws on is the one the reader is looking at, which need not be the map showing
        here — so it is last of the two, the further of them from the tabs that say which map this
        is. GM-only, and absent rather than disabled for a player: it writes a Scene flag, which a
        player cannot do, and a control that is visibly there and refuses on click is worse than one
        that never offered. Players still SEE the line, because the flag broadcasts. Built only once
        there is a route to draw. See utils/scene-route.js. --}}
  {{#if journey.scene}}
  <button type="button" class="stonetop-journey-to-scene{{#if journey.scene.showing}} is-showing{{/if}}"
          data-tooltip="{{journey.scene.tooltip}}">
    <i class="fas {{journey.scene.icon}}"></i> {{journey.scene.label}}
  </button>
  {{/if}}
</div>
